Maya C: FYI we've got a customer on prod hitting the rerank endpoint and getting either a 400/422 or plausible 200 but all scores very low. They believe it's because of how they nest documents -> passages -> candidates and the query field name. They pasted their payload but it's confusing. Can eng help triage? Customer is on Premium and this is impacting live UX. :sob:

Arjun P: Can you paste the exact payload + the error body they're seeing? Also which SDK/version and whether they're using POST /v1/rerank or the batch endpoint.

Maya C: Pasting redacted payload (customer supplied):
```json
{
  "query": "how to refund an invoice",
  "documents": [
    {
      "id": "doc_1",
      "title": "billing faq",
      "passages": [
        {"text": "refund policy is 30 days", "start": 0},
        {"text": "contact support for exceptions", "start": 45}
      ],
      "candidates": [
        {"text": "we issue refunds within 30 days", "score": 0.2},
        {"text": "refund exceptions handled by finance", "score": 0.1}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "doc_2",
      "passages": [
        {"text": "refunds are not allowed for digital goods"}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "candidates": [
    {"text": "contact billing@company.com"},
    {"text": "open a support ticket"}
  ]
}
```

Maya C: Error response they saw (copied):
```json
{
  "error": "input schema ambiguous: found both top-level candidates and candidates nested under documents; ambiguous mapping to ranking items",
  "code": 422
}
```

Maya C: Customer says when they flatten everything into top-level `candidates` it works but they need the document->passage mapping to surface source ids in the UI (they rely on document ids).

Jasper L: Repro steps for eng: 1) POST payload above to /v1/rerank 2) note 422 response OR 200 with scores like 0.01/0.02 for all items. I can see in the runtime logs we're doing a validation pass that flattens nested passages into candidate items but if both nested `candidates` and top-level `candidates` present we currently treat it as conflicting and reject.

Jasper L: From the logs (sanitized):
```
validation: payload v1 -> nested_documents=true, top_level_candidates=true
validator: ambiguous_candidate_source -> reject (422) context={customer=acct_123, endpoint=rerank}
runtime: fallback to scoring_flattened_candidates -> scores=[0.010,0.012,...] (when forced through old path)
```

Arjun P: Ok that explains the 422. But why do they sometimes get a 200 with low scores? Sounds like a code path that silently flattens nested passages into candidates but assigns weak priors. That would explain plausible but useless results. We should make behavior deterministic: either error with clear guidance or accept and normalize with `document_id` preserved.

Teresa N: Docs note: our public schema shows `documents[]` containing `passages[]` but examples for rerank show top-level `candidates[]`. I think customers conflate the two. We should clarify examples and add an explicit note about allowed combinations. Also update error text to say: "Use either top-level candidates OR nested documents+passages; if you need mapping include `document_id` on each candidate or use `documents` with `passages` only."

Maya C: Customer is asking for a minimal non-breaking workaround they can ship now. Jasper, can you propose one I can pass to them?

Jasper L: Workaround: flatten into top-level candidates but keep `document_id` and `passage_index` metadata on each candidate so UI can reconstruct origin. Example payload that should work today:
```json
{
  "query": "how to refund an invoice",
  "candidates": [
    {"text":"we issue refunds within 30 days","document_id":"doc_1","passage_index":0},
    {"text":"contact support for exceptions","document_id":"doc_1","passage_index":1},
    {"text":"refunds are not allowed for digital goods","document_id":"doc_2","passage_index":0},
    {"text":"contact billing@company.com","document_id":"doc_external","passage_index":0}
  ]
}
```

Jasper L: That preserves document mapping and avoids ambiguous schema. We should offer a small snippet for common languages showing flattening script.

Arjun P: Technical fix proposals: (A) Update validator to accept nested documents + top-level candidates if top-level candidates include `origin` metadata (document_id or source) and auto-normalize into canonical candidates list; (B) Otherwise return 400 with a helpful message and example. Also add a runtime metric to track ambiguous_payloads. I'll open a PR for (B) first (fast), leave (A) as follow-up since it affects ingestion/backcompat.

Teresa N: I'll update the docs and create a short migration guide + snippet for Ruby/JS/Python flatteners. Also suggest adding a dedicated example in the rerank reference showing `documents` -> `passages` only and another showing `candidates` with `document_id` metadata.

Maya C: Customer is asking for ETA — can we push the workaround instructions now and promise a clearer error message within 48h? Priority: high because UX is broken on prod.

Jasper L: I'll DM the customer the flattening payload + a toy Python snippet and mark as workaround. ETA for docs update and improved error text: 48h.

Arjun P: I'll open a quick validator PR today to detect ambiguous payloads and return 400 with this message: "Ambiguous candidate sources: include only `documents` (with `passages`) OR top-level `candidates`. To preserve document mapping, add `document_id` to each candidate. See link to migration guide." Will link to Teresa's doc once published.

infra-bot: Incident created #INC-2026-421: rerank-schema-ambiguity affecting acct_123. Assigned to @arjunp, @jasperl, @teresann, notify: #customer-success.

Maya C: Thanks — please post PR/links here and I'll keep the customer updated. Also flagging billing/contact ops in case this gets escalated. :raised_hands:
